---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title='"Tell me about service objects."' description="Practice card. The interviewer wants more than a definition; they want the trade-off. Drills the standard senior answer.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/practice" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Practice
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Interview · Card 2</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">"Tell me about service objects."</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">A senior answer doesn't just define them. It names when not to use them. Pick the answer the interviewer is looking for.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The question
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">"What's a service object, and when would you use one?" Which is the answer that signals senior?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'A service object is a PORO with a <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">call</code> method that encapsulates a business operation. Use one whenever a controller action has more than ten lines.',
              explanation: 'The definition is right; the rule is too rigid. Ten lines isn\'t the threshold; "the action mixes multiple responsibilities that benefit from being separately testable" is. A 30-line action that\'s coherent and only used in one place is fine.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: 'A service object is a PORO that owns one business operation, exposed via a <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">call</code> method. Use one when the operation has multiple side effects that need to be coordinated, when the operation is invoked from more than one place, or when isolating the operation makes it testable without booting controllers. Don\'t reach for one if the action is short, single-purpose, and called from one place — that\'s premature abstraction.',
              explanation: 'This is the senior answer. Definition + when to use + when <em>not</em> to use. The "when not to" sentence is what scores. Note: this also names the 37signals school\'s view implicitly — they often prefer rich domain models over service objects, so the "we don\'t always do this" framing matters.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'A service object replaces controller actions. They handle HTTP requests and render responses, so the controllers can stay thin.',
              explanation: 'Service objects don\'t handle HTTP. They\'re called by controllers. The controller still parses params, calls the service, renders the response.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'A service object is an ActiveRecord model with no table backing it. It uses ActiveRecord callbacks to coordinate side effects.',
              explanation: 'No. ActiveRecord callbacks are exactly the thing service objects often move <em>away</em> from. A service object is a plain Ruby class; AR involvement is a smell here.',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>Senior answers always end with a trade-off or a caveat. "Use X when Y, don\'t use X when Z." It signals: you\'ve seen the pattern overused; you know the cost of indirection; you can be trusted to make the call in either direction.</p><p>Bonus framing: name the two schools. The "Mastodon / Forem / Gumroad" school extracts service objects aggressively. The "37signals / Hey" school keeps logic on the model. Both are senior. A good interviewer hears either framing and notices that you know the other exists.</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/senior-srp-service-objects"
          theoryLabel="SOLID · SRP · Service Objects"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-interview-includes-variants" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 1</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-interview-n-plus-one" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 3 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
